## Why memoisation exists at all

Because a parent re-render re-renders every descendant, a state change near the root of a big tree can call thousands of component functions. Most of them return the same output, so the commit does nothing, but the render work still costs main-thread time and shows up as poor Interaction to Next Paint (INP). Memoisation lets React **skip** rendering subtrees whose inputs did not change.

React gives you three manual tools and, since 2025, a compiler that applies them for you.

| Tool | Memoises | Skips | Breaks when |
|---|---|---|---|
| `memo(Component)` | The rendered output of a component | Re-rendering if every prop is `Object.is` equal | Any prop is a new object, array or function each render |
| `useMemo(fn, deps)` | A computed value | Recomputing when deps are unchanged | Deps change every render; or the computation is cheap anyway |
| `useCallback(fn, deps)` | A function identity | Creating a new function reference | Only useful when the identity matters (a `memo` child, an effect dep) |

`useCallback(fn, deps)` is just `useMemo(() => fn, deps)`. Neither makes the function or computation faster. They only preserve identity or skip work across renders.

## Fix the structure before you memoise

Dan Abramov's "Before You memo()" shows two refactors that remove unnecessary re-renders without any memoisation, and they still matter with the compiler because they make the data flow clearer.

**1. Move state down.** If only a small part of a big component uses some state, extract that part into its own component so the state change re-renders only it.

```text
BEFORE: App owns `color` -> every keystroke re-renders App -> ExpensiveTree re-renders
AFTER:  ColorPicker owns `color` -> keystrokes re-render ColorPicker only
```

**2. Lift content up (children as props).** When the stateful wrapper must sit above the expensive part, pass the expensive part as `children`. The `children` element was created by the parent, which did not re-render, so React sees the same element object and skips it.

```tsx
function ColorBox({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  const [color, setColor] = useState('red');
  return (
    <div style={{ color }}>
      <input value={color} onChange={(e) => setColor(e.target.value)} />
      {children /* same element reference as last render: not re-rendered */}
    </div>
  );
}
// <ColorBox><ExpensiveTree /></ColorBox>
```

**3. Split contexts.** Every consumer of a context re-renders when its value changes, and `memo` cannot stop that. Putting `{ user, theme, cart }` in one provider means a cart update re-renders everything that reads the theme. Split by update frequency, memoise the provider value, or use an external store with selectors (`useSyncExternalStore`, Zustand, Redux) so components subscribe to slices.

## Manual memoisation done right

```tsx
const Row = memo(function Row({ item, onSelect }: RowProps) {
  return <li onClick={() => onSelect(item.id)}>{item.label}</li>;
});

function List({ items, query }: { items: Item[]; query: string }) {
  const [selected, setSelected] = useState<string | null>(null);
  const visible = useMemo(() => items.filter((i) => i.label.includes(query)), [items, query]);
  const onSelect = useCallback((id: string) => setSelected(id), []); // setState is stable
  return <ul>{visible.map((i) => <Row key={i.id} item={i} onSelect={onSelect} />)}</ul>;
}
```

The pieces depend on each other. `memo(Row)` is useless if `onSelect` is a fresh arrow function every render, and `useCallback` is useless if `Row` is not memoised. That fragility, where one inline object deep in a prop chain silently defeats the whole optimisation, is the main argument for letting a compiler do it.

When manual memoisation is **not** worth it:

- The component is cheap and the tree below it is small. The comparison itself costs something.
- Props are new on every render anyway (data from a fetch without structural sharing).
- You have not measured. The React DevTools Profiler "Highlight updates" option and the flame chart show which components render and how long they take.

## The React Compiler

The **React Compiler** (stable 1.0 since October 2025) is a build-time Babel plugin (`babel-plugin-react-compiler`) that analyses components and hooks and inserts memoisation automatically. Next.js 16 supports it with `reactCompiler: true` in `next.config.ts`, and Vite, Expo and other toolchains have integrations. It works with React 19 directly and with React 17 and 18 through the `react-compiler-runtime` package.

What it does, conceptually: it builds a data-flow graph of your component, figures out which values depend on which props and state, and caches each value and JSX subtree so it is recomputed only when its own inputs change. The output looks roughly like this:

```ts
// You write:
function Greeting({ user, onLogout }) {
  const name = formatName(user);
  return <Header title={name} onLogout={onLogout} />;
}

// The compiler emits something like (simplified):
function Greeting(props) {
  const $ = _c(4); // a per-instance cache slot array
  let name;
  if ($[0] !== props.user) { name = formatName(props.user); $[0] = props.user; $[1] = name; }
  else { name = $[1]; }
  let jsx;
  if ($[2] !== name || $[3] !== props.onLogout) { jsx = <Header title={name} onLogout={props.onLogout} />; /* store */ }
  else { /* reuse cached jsx */ }
  return jsx;
}
```

Because it caches the **JSX element**, a child receiving the same element is skipped exactly as with `memo`, without you wrapping anything. It can also memoise after an early return or inside branches, which manual hooks cannot do because hooks cannot be called conditionally.

### What it requires: the Rules of React

The compiler is only correct for code that follows the Rules of React: components and hooks are pure during render, props and state are treated as immutable, hooks are called unconditionally at the top level, and values passed to JSX are not mutated afterwards. When it detects a violation it can see, it **skips that component** and leaves it as written rather than miscompiling it. Violations it cannot see (mutating an object from a module scope during render, reading a ref's `.current` during render) can still cause stale UI.

Practical adoption notes:

- Enable the compiler-aware rules in `eslint-plugin-react-hooks` first and fix what they report. That is where most of the value is, even before turning the compiler on.
- Roll out incrementally (a directory or an annotation mode), and opt individual components out with the `"use no memo"` directive while debugging.
- Existing `useMemo`/`useCallback` can stay. The compiler works alongside them, though new code rarely needs them.
- It does not fix architectural problems: a context holding the whole app state still re-renders all consumers on every change, and a list of 10,000 DOM rows is still slow to commit. It removes **unnecessary renders**, not necessary work.

## Effects are not part of rendering

A large class of "too many renders" bugs is effects used to derive state:

```tsx
// BAD: renders once with stale fullName, then the effect sets state and renders again
const [fullName, setFullName] = useState('');
useEffect(() => setFullName(`${first} ${last}`), [first, last]);

// GOOD: derive during render (the compiler will memoise it if it is expensive)
const fullName = `${first} ${last}`;
```

The react.dev page "You Might Not Need an Effect" lists the patterns: derive values during render, reset state with a `key`, handle user actions in event handlers, and use effects only to synchronise with something outside React (a subscription, a non-React widget, the network). Every unnecessary effect that sets state adds a full extra render and commit, and in chains it adds several.

## Measuring render cost

1. **React DevTools Profiler.** Record an interaction. The flame chart shows each committed render, which components rendered, how long each took, and "why did this render?" (enable it in settings): hooks changed, props changed, parent rendered.
2. **Chrome Performance panel.** React 19.2 adds custom tracks for Scheduler and Components to the Performance panel, so you can see React's priorities and component render times next to the browser's own work.
3. **`<Profiler onRender>`** in code, for logging render durations of a subtree in production builds that enable profiling.

Look for components that render often with nothing changed, and for single renders over a few milliseconds. Fix structure first, then memoise the hot spots, or let the compiler handle the general case.

